mode: "100644"
text: "# Analytics & reporting 2026 — verified\n\n*Volatile. Re-verify quarterly (benchmarks/metric names shift).*\n\n## Vanity vs actionable (the core)\nMost teams **track the wrong numbers** — celebrating 10k new followers while engagement drops 40%.\nOnly ~**4–6 KPIs actually correlate with revenue** for most businesses, yet the average dashboard shows\n20+. **Vanity metrics** (total followers, total impressions, total likes) look impressive, are **easily\ninflated/faked**, and don't map to outcomes. The **three tests** for a real metric: (1) Can it **inform\na decision**? (2) Does it **correlate with a goal/revenue**? (3) Is it **hard to manipulate**? **If a\nmetric won't change what you do next week, it's a diagnostic — not a headline KPI.** Pick **3–5 that\nmatter.**\n\n## The KPI stack (map the funnel)\nFour layers: **Distribution** (reach, impressions, frequency, follower-growth-rate — \"did the algorithm\ngive you a chance\") → **Attention** (avg watch time, completion rate, **saves per 1k reach**, **shares\nper 1k reach** — \"did the creative earn time\") → **Action** (CTR, CVR, CPA, ROAS, RPM — \"did it move\npeople\") → **Risk** (fraud/authenticity/misleading-reporting guard). Map **goal → 1 primary KPI + ~2\nsupporting**, and fix the **measurement window** (7 / 28-day / campaign) so it's apples-to-apples.\n\n## The signal metrics (2026, over likes)\n- **Saves & shares** — better leading indicators than likes; signal **intent + relevance** (saves = \"I'll\n  return\"; shares = sent to non-followers = discovery).\n- **Video:** **average watch time, completion rate, audience retention, drop-off** (track in 25%\n  increments; YouTube ~70% avg watch ≈ exceptional).\n- **Engagement rate by reach** (not by followers, not raw totals) — accounts for algorithmic reach.\n- **Follower growth RATE** `(new ÷ starting)×100` — resonance/health, not raw count.\n- **CTR / link clicks** and **social-attributed conversions** (UTM + GA4) for traffic/sales.\n\n## Platform-specific signal (optimise the right one)\nEach platform rewards a different action — optimising the wrong one is wasted effort: **Instagram →\nsaves**; **TikTok → shares**; **LinkedIn → dwell time + saves**; **YouTube → watch time/retention**;\n**Pinterest → outbound clicks/saves**; **X → replies/bookmarks**; **Facebook → saves/shares**.\n\n## Benchmarks — relative, not absolute (verify-quarterly)\nA \"2% engagement rate\" means nothing without context. **Benchmark vs your own trend over time and your\nvertical/competitor set**, not platform averages. Rough 2026 \"strong\" ER ranges (vary widely): IG ~3–6%\nbrand, TikTok ~5–10%, LinkedIn ~2–4% personal / ~0.5–1.5% company. SaaS/B2B on visual platforms runs\nlower — expected, not failure.\n\n## Attribution + privacy (honest limits)\nCookieless era → **first-party data essential** (email, pixels, consented tracking). Use **UTM + GA4\nconversion events**. **Assume social attribution undercounts true impact by ~30–50%** (last/first-touch\nmiss the journey) — read **directional** signals, not false precision.\n\n## Reporting\n**Flip the hierarchy:** lead with **business outcomes**, use content metrics to explain *why*. A monthly\nreport should take ~**30 min to compile / 10 to present** on a **standardized template**; its most\nimportant section is **Recommendations — 3 specific next actions.** **Tier by cadence:** weekly pulse\n(top posts, completion, best times → next batch) · monthly trends · quarterly strategy. **Don't overreact**\nto one weak post or strong week. Set metric **definitions** early (they drift across platforms/agencies).\n"
